faq:
  - q: "How does career coaching help after a layoff?"
    a: "Three ways: (1) Positioning — a coach helps you build the resume, LinkedIn, and narrative that presents your experience at its strongest, removing the uncertainty that a layoff can create in how hiring managers read your background. (2) Strategy — a job search after a layoff is time-sensitive; a coach helps you prioritize the highest-ROI channels (network, recruiter outreach, targeted applications) and avoid the low-conversion approaches (mass applying, LinkedIn Easy Apply). (3) Emotional calibration — a layoff creates psychological pressure that distorts decision-making. Coaching provides an outside perspective that prevents underpricing yourself, targeting too broadly, or accepting the wrong role out of urgency."
  - q: "Should I take time to process the layoff before starting my search?"
    a: "Start the search immediately — even if you need processing time. You can always decline opportunities. You cannot undo weeks of delay in a competitive market. The practical actions (updating LinkedIn, contacting your network, updating your resume) take hours, not days. The emotional processing happens in parallel, not instead of the search. Professionals who wait 4–6 weeks before starting their search consistently take longer to land than those who start within the first two weeks."
  - q: "What should I tell people about my layoff?"
    a: "The truth, stated neutrally and briefly. Layoffs are common, well-understood, and not a negative signal to most experienced hiring managers. The statement: 'The company went through a restructuring that affected my team' is sufficient. Follow it immediately with what you are looking for — that is the conversation you want to be having. Do not over-explain, criticize the company, or frame it as a shock. State it, pivot forward."
  - q: "How do I avoid taking a lower-level role or lower salary because of layoff pressure?"
    a: "Know your market rate before any offer arrives. Salary research (Levels.fyi, LinkedIn Salary, Glassdoor, peer conversations) gives you an anchor that is independent of your urgency to land. When an offer arrives below market, you can negotiate from data: 'Based on my research and the market for this role and level, I was expecting something closer to [target]. Is there flexibility?' Your financial pressure is not visible to the other side — and should not be. Decisions made from urgency rather than data are the source of most post-layoff career regrets."
  - q: "Is a layoff an opportunity to change careers?"
    a: "It can be — but treat it as one option, not the automatic response. A career change adds 6–18 months to your landing timeline and requires deliberate skill transfer positioning. If you were already planning a career change before the layoff, the forced transition accelerates that plan. If the career change idea is new — born from frustration rather than genuine long-term planning — evaluate it carefully before adding complexity to an already difficult search."
